<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>$(DEVELOPMENT_LANGUAGE)</string>
	<!-- The nine languages this app ships. The catalogs live in the
	     RelayiumAppKit package bundle rather than this bundle, so the list keeps
	     the app's declared localizations in step with AppLanguage. The macOS
	     scene also applies that resolved language's layout direction explicitly:
	     CFBundleLocalizations alone did not mirror a package-backed Arabic UI on
	     a real build. The release-readiness script checks this list. -->
	<key>CFBundleLocalizations</key>
	<array>
		<string>en</string>
		<string>zh-Hans</string>
		<string>ja</string>
		<string>ko</string>
		<string>de</string>
		<string>fr</string>
		<string>ar</string>
		<string>es</string>
		<string>pt</string>
	</array>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
	<!-- Written by hand rather than left to actool's partial-plist merge. With
	     GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE = NO and a hand-maintained INFOPLIST_FILE, the
	     key would otherwise arrive only by that merge — and a silent failure of
	     it is indistinguishable from a missing icon. One line makes the
	     acceptance check unambiguous. The name matches
	     ASSETCATALOG_COMPILER_APPICON_NAME in both build configurations. -->
	<key>CFBundleIconName</key>
	<string>AppIcon</string>
	<!-- What makes Relayium a Dock drop target and a Finder "Open With" entry.
	     Without it the app has no way in from outside its own window, which is
	     the native advantage a desktop app has over the website and the reason
	     this exists at all.

	     `LSHandlerRank` = `Alternate` is the load-bearing line, not boilerplate,
	     and it is set from a measurement rather than from reasoning. This entry
	     shipped at `None` on the belief that `None` still honoured an explicit
	     Open With. On 2026-08-07 the owner installed the notarized 1.0 build in
	     /Applications and Finder's Open With menu did not list Relayium at all;
	     the installed plist confirmed `None` as the cause. `None` does not mean
	     "never propose me but honour an explicit choice" — on the shipped macOS
	     behaviour it excluded the app from the menu outright.

	     `Alternate` is the rank that makes Relayium an explicit secondary
	     candidate: it appears in Open With below the app that owns the type,
	     without being offered as that type's default. `Owner` is what would
	     claim primary ownership, and it is rejected — Relayium transports these
	     items, and a utility that became the Mac's default opener for every file
	     because it once claimed `public.data` is a support incident, not a
	     feature. Nothing here changes what a double-click does.

	     The content types stay `public.data` and `public.folder` — deliberately
	     everything, because "send this" is not a format question and a list of
	     extensions would be a list of the files a user may NOT send. That breadth
	     is why the rank must stay at `Alternate` and never rise to `Owner`.

	     `Viewer`, not `Editor`: Relayium never writes back to what it is given.
	     It reads the bytes, encrypts them and sends them; the original file is
	     untouched, and `Editor` would additionally make this app a candidate for
	     "revert to" and document-restore behaviour it does not implement.

	     `CFBundleTypeName` is not localized. `Alternate` with a `Viewer` role
	     does not make Relayium the owner of `public.data` or `public.folder`, so
	     the places a type name is read — the Kind column, Get Info — are expected
	     to keep answering with the real owner's name, and nine InfoPlist.strings
	     would be nine translations of a string this app is not expected to
	     display. That expectation has not been re-measured on an installed
	     `Alternate` build; the installed Open With acceptance that is still
	     required is the place to check it. -->
	<key>CFBundleDocumentTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleTypeName</key>
			<string>File or folder to send</string>
			<key>CFBundleTypeRole</key>
			<string>Viewer</string>
			<key>LSHandlerRank</key>
			<string>Alternate</string>
			<key>LSItemContentTypes</key>
			<array>
				<string>public.data</string>
				<string>public.folder</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>$(MARKETING_VERSION)</string>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>$(CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION)</string>
	<key>LSApplicationCategoryType</key>
	<string>public.app-category.utilities</string>
	<key>LSMinimumSystemVersion</key>
	<string>$(MACOSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET)</string>
	<key>NSHumanReadableCopyright</key>
	<string></string>
	<key>SUEnableInstallerLauncherService</key>
	<true/>
	<key>SUFeedURL</key>
	<string>https://relayium.com/apps/macos/appcast.xml</string>
	<key>SUPublicEDKey</key>
	<string>AoA0oNo2cZzjIJ4CJgnaFUhy3EAucSxcM+/iayLux6k=</string>
</dict>
</plist>
